GLOSSARY = [
 ("Agent","An AI system that can plan steps and use tools (search, code, apps) to complete a task with some autonomy."),
 ("Alignment","Work to make AI systems behave according to human intentions and values."),
 ("Algorithm","A set of step-by-step rules a computer follows to solve a problem."),
 ("API","Application Programming Interface — a way for software to send requests to an AI model and receive answers."),
 ("Artificial intelligence (AI)","Computer systems performing tasks that normally need human intelligence, such as understanding language or recognising images."),
 ("Attention","The transformer mechanism that lets a model weigh how relevant each part of the input is to every other part."),
 ("Backpropagation","The method that works out how much each weight contributed to an error so it can be adjusted during training."),
 ("Benchmark","A standard test used to compare AI models' performance."),
 ("Bias","Systematic unfairness in AI outputs, often inherited from training data or design choices."),
 ("Chain-of-thought","Prompting or training a model to work through intermediate reasoning steps before answering."),
 ("Chatbot","Software you interact with through conversation; modern chatbots are often powered by LLMs."),
 ("Chunking","Splitting documents into smaller passages so they can be searched and retrieved."),
 ("Classification","Assigning an input to a category, such as spam or not spam."),
 ("Computer vision","AI that interprets images and video."),
 ("Context window","The maximum amount of text (in tokens) a model can consider at once."),
 ("Dataset","A collection of examples used to train or test a model."),
 ("Deep learning","Machine learning using neural networks with many layers."),
 ("Diffusion model","A type of generative model, common for images, that learns to turn noise into a picture step by step."),
 ("Embedding","A list of numbers representing the meaning of text or images so similar items sit close together."),
 ("Evaluation (evals)","Systematic testing of AI outputs against a test set and scoring rubric."),
 ("Few-shot prompting","Including a few examples in a prompt to show the desired output."),
 ("Fine-tuning","Further training a pre-trained model on specific examples to specialise its behaviour."),
 ("Foundation model","A large model trained on broad data that can be adapted to many tasks."),
 ("Generative AI","AI that creates new content such as text, images, audio or code."),
 ("GPU","Graphics processing unit — hardware widely used to train and run AI models."),
 ("Gradient descent","An optimisation method that repeatedly adjusts weights to reduce error."),
 ("Grounding","Tying a model's answer to specific trusted sources."),
 ("Guardrails","Rules, filters or checks that keep AI behaviour within acceptable limits."),
 ("Hallucination","A fluent, confident AI output that is false or unsupported."),
 ("Human in the loop","A design where a person reviews or approves AI outputs before they take effect."),
 ("Inference","Running a trained model to produce an output."),
 ("Large language model (LLM)","A neural network trained on large amounts of text to predict and generate language."),
 ("Latency","How long a model takes to respond."),
 ("Machine learning","Systems that learn patterns from data rather than following only hand-written rules."),
 ("Model","The trained mathematical system that turns inputs into outputs."),
 ("Multimodal","Able to work with more than one kind of data, such as text, images and audio."),
 ("Natural language processing (NLP)","The field of AI concerned with understanding and generating human language."),
 ("Neural network","A model made of layers of connected units whose weights are learned from data."),
 ("Open-weight model","A model whose trained weights are published so others can run or adapt it."),
 ("Overfitting","When a model memorises training data and performs poorly on new data."),
 ("Parameter","An internal value (weight) learned during training; large models have billions."),
 ("Pre-training","The initial large-scale training of a model on broad data."),
 ("Prompt","The instruction and context you give an AI model."),
 ("Prompt chaining","Breaking a task into a sequence of prompts, each using the previous output."),
 ("Prompt engineering","The practice of designing prompts that produce reliable, useful outputs."),
 ("Prompt injection","Hidden or malicious instructions in content that try to hijack an AI system's behaviour."),
 ("Reasoning model","A model trained to spend more computation working through problems step by step before answering."),
 ("Reinforcement learning","Learning by trial and error using rewards."),
 ("RLHF","Reinforcement learning from human feedback — using human preference ratings to shape model behaviour."),
 ("Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)","Retrieving relevant documents and giving them to a model so it answers from those sources."),
 ("Semantic search","Search based on meaning rather than exact keywords, often using embeddings."),
 ("Supervised learning","Training on examples paired with correct answers."),
 ("System prompt","Background instructions that set an AI assistant's behaviour for a whole conversation."),
 ("Temperature","A setting controlling how random or varied a model's outputs are."),
 ("Token","A chunk of text — a word, part of a word or punctuation — that models read and write."),
 ("Training data","The examples a model learns from."),
 ("Transformer","The neural-network architecture behind most modern language models, built around attention."),
 ("Unsupervised learning","Finding patterns in data without labelled answers."),
 ("Vector database","A database designed to store embeddings and search them by similarity."),
 ("Weights","The learned numbers inside a neural network that determine its behaviour."),
 ("Zero-shot prompting","Asking a model to do a task without giving examples."),
]
